---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ProvincialFilter from "../components/ProvincialFilter.astro";
import { translations } from "../i18n/translations";

const t = translations.en;
---

<Layout title={t.financial.title} lang="en">
  <div class="mb-8 border-b pb-6">
    <h1
      class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
    >
      {t.financial.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600">
      {t.financial.subtitle}
    </p>
  </div>

  <ProvincialFilter lang="en" />

  <article class="prose max-w-none mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
    <h2>1. Provincial Healthcare Coverage: What to Expect</h2>
    <p>
      In Canada, provincial healthcare systems (such as OHIP in Ontario, RAMQ in
      Quebec, and MSP in British Columbia) operate under the <em
        >Canada Health Act</em
      >. This means that <strong
        >medically necessary services provided in a hospital are fully covered</strong
      >.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>In-Hospital Treatments:</strong> Chemotherapy, radiation, cellular
        therapies, and surgeries administered while your child is admitted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Out-of-Pocket Expenses:</strong> Take-home prescriptions, hospital
        parking, and travel/accommodation when traveling to specialized tertiary centers.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Employment Insurance (EI): Family Caregiver Benefit for Children</h2>
    <p>
      The Government of Canada offers the <strong
        >Family Caregiver Benefit for Children</strong
      > to help offset lost income when parents take time away from work:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Duration:</strong> Up to <strong>35 weeks</strong> of benefits, shareable
        between eligible caregivers over a 52-week window.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Eligibility:</strong> A reduction of more than 40% in regular weekly
        earnings and 600 insurable hours accumulated in the preceding 52 weeks.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Provincial Drug Coverage for Out-of-Hospital Medications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Ontario (OHIP+ & Trillium):</strong> OHIP+ covers 5,000+ prescription
        drugs for youth 24 and under without private plans. Trillium helps households
        with high prescription costs relative to income.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quebec (RAMQ):</strong> Public Prescription Drug Insurance Plan for
        families without private group insurance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>British Columbia (Fair PharmaCare):</strong> Income-based assistance
        covering eligible take-home prescriptions.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. First Nations, Inuit, and Métis Health Support & NIHB</h2>
    <p>
      Indigenous Services Canada (ISC) administers the <strong
        >Non-Insured Health Benefits (NIHB)</strong
      > program, fully covering medical travel, airfare, meals, and lodging for the
      child and an <strong>authorized caregiver escort</strong>, as well as 100%
      prescription drug coverage.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Specialized Charities and Financial Grants</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>POGO Financial Assistance (POFAP - Ontario):</strong> Reimburses out-of-pocket
        hospital food, accommodations, and childcare for siblings under 12.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leucan (Quebec):</strong> Direct emergency grants, hospital massage
        therapy, and school reintegration.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Childhood Cancer Canada:</strong> National emergency fund and post-secondary
        survivor scholarships.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
